a rare televised hearing in Israel's  High Court allowing the public to watch  for themselves the latest round of the  political crisis they've been living  with for more than a year after three  elections will there finally be a  government this week the first issue is  the assignment of a Knesset member to  form a government against whom charges  have been filed five out of eight  appeals are concerning this issue the  second issue has to do with the validity  of the coalition agreement that was  signed on April 20th that agreement for  a rotating co-equal Premiership between  Benjamin Netanyahu and his former rival  Benny Gantz requires changes to Israel's  fundamental basic laws to allow each  mistrustful partner the guarantees they  want to ensure the deal sticks Sunday  though saw the focus on the other issue  whether Benjamin Netanyahu indicted in  three separate corruption cases could  legally form and lead a new government  the law requires ministers in such a  position to resign but not Prime  Minister's Netanyahu is both a caretaker  Prime Minister and a candidate for the  job leaving room for legal argument  during it the justices seemed to signal  that they would not overrule what they  see as the will of the members of  Israel's parliament not a message  popular with anti Netanyahu  anti-coalition protesters who'd gathered  to watch proceedings in a park lying  symbolically between the court and the  Parliament as well as the critical  juncture in Israel's long-running  political crisis this is a major moment  for its constitution Netanyahu's legal  team argues that the Knesset the Israeli  Parliament has made its decision and the  court should not intervene his opponents  argue that allowing for a government to  be led by a man under such serious  criminal charges represents an  earthquake to the rule of law it's now  up to the justices to decide for now  Gantz and Netanyahu are still working to  pass the legislation required to form  the new government before the deadline  expires on Thursday if that doesn't  happen or if the court were to surprise  everyone and rule it out then Israel  would be  for a fourth election in less than 18  months with its political system  straining at the scenes  Harry Fawcett al Jazeera West Jerusalem
 

